{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1087", "sample_idx": 26, "model_name": "stockmark/stockmark-2-100b-instruct", "content": "the social network is a 2010 biographical drama film directed by david fincher and written by aphonso brooks, based on the wikipedia article \"facebook\" by evan wright. the movie focuses on the early days of facebook and the legal battles faced by mark zuckerberg and the company. the film begins with the complicated relationship between zuckerberg and his friend edward winklevoss, and how this led to the creation of facebook.\n\nthe performances in the social network are exceptional. jesse Eisenberg delivers a powerful portrayal of mark zuckerberg, capturing the complexities and nuances of the character. the supporting cast, including andrew garfield as edward winklevoss, jake gyllenhaal as douglas paulson, and max chrisley as cody goodwin, also put in strong performances, adding depth and credibility to their characters.\n\nthe film's direction by david fincher is masterful. he manages to weave together the legal and personal narratives seamlessly, keeping the audience engaged throughout the film. the visual style is sleek and modern, fitting the story's subject matter. the score by thom yorke is haunting and adds a layer of tension and emotion to the film.\n\nthe social network is not just a biopic; it is a sharp and incisive commentary on the era of social media and the impact it has had on our lives. it explores themes of ambition, loyalty, and the cost of success. while the film does not shy away from portraying zuckerberg as a flawed individual, it also highlights his ingenuity and determination.\n\nthe social network is a must-watch for anyone interested in the story of facebook or the tech industry as a whole. it is a well-crafted film that is both entertaining and thought-provoking. the movie provides a fascinating look into the world of start-ups and the legal challenges they face, while also delving into the personal lives of the 